# DocuComply AI

**LLM-based document extraction with deterministic compliance validation.**

DocuComply AI extracts structured data from Mexican IMSS/SUA payroll documents
using an LLM, then verifies that output with deterministic business rules and
measures it against human-written ground truth.

> AI extracts. Code verifies. Humans review uncertainty.

---

## Why this project exists

This year I shipped a production system that processes IMSS/SUA documents in
C#/.NET — text extraction, parsing rules, OCR for scanned files, and
deterministic validation. Working with those documents taught me exactly where
that approach strains: layouts drift between employers, rows break across
lines, and every new edge case becomes another hardcoded rule.

When I started working with LLMs, rather than building a demo from scratch I
went back to the problem I already knew and built a second implementation of
it. The goal was to compare both approaches with data: how well a model
reconstructs a malformed document, where it fails, how far its output can be
trusted, and which validations remain necessary even with a model in the loop.

The result is not an attempt to replace deterministic rules with a model. It is
a hybrid architecture — the model provides flexibility in interpreting
documents, deterministic code verifies whatever must be verifiable — with a
ground-truth harness so the comparison rests on measurement rather than
assumption.

---

## The problem

Traditional document parsers fail visibly when layouts change. LLMs behave
differently: they reconstruct malformed information surprisingly well, but an
incorrect inference looks just as convincing as a correct one.

For compliance-oriented processing that raises a specific question:

**How do you use the flexibility of a model without blindly trusting its
output?**

This project answers it with layers that check different things:

- The model interprets documents and produces structured output.
- Deterministic code validates business rules and internal consistency.
- Human-created ground truth measures extraction accuracy.
- An explicit `anomalies` field surfaces model inference instead of hiding it.
- Extraction routes are measured independently rather than assumed equivalent.

---

## Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Payroll Document]

    A --> B[Route A: PDF Text Layer]
    A --> C[Route B: Page Images]

    B --> D[LLM Structured Extraction]
    C --> D

    D --> E[Typed Payroll Data]
    E --> F[Deterministic Validation]

    F --> G{Result}
    G -->|Valid| H[PASS]
    G -->|Suspicious| I[WARNING]
    G -->|Unsafe / Invalid| J[HUMAN REVIEW]

    E --> K[Evaluation Harness]
    L[Human Ground Truth] --> K
    K --> M[Field-Level Accuracy]
```

Extraction, validation and evaluation are deliberately separate concerns,
because they answer different questions:

| Layer | Question it answers |
|---|---|
| Model | What does this document appear to contain? |
| Validator | Are these extracted values internally consistent? |
| Evaluation harness | Do these values match human-verified ground truth? |

No single mechanism should answer all three.

---

## Design decisions

### Work locations as a first-class level

The concept came from the original system, where one requirement was to filter
and separate information by work location. Carrying it into DocuComply made
clear this was not only a presentation concern: it is how the source document
is actually structured. Each location holds its own employees and its own
printed subtotal.

Modeling it as a flat list of employees would have been simpler and would have
lost the ability to reconcile at two levels — first that employee contributions
sum to their location's stated subtotal, then that location subtotals sum to
the document's grand total. The hierarchy both mirrors the real document and
enables a validation rule that would not otherwise exist.

### Stated totals are transcribed, never recalculated

The schema instructs the model to copy printed totals and subtotals verbatim.
If the model recalculated them, validation would be comparing one computed sum
against another computed sum — the document's own claim would disappear from
the comparison, and inconsistencies in the source would become invisible.

### Schema descriptions are prompt, not documentation

Field descriptions in `schema.ts` are written to steer model behavior
directly — for example, *"Keep leading zeros. Never reformat."* on the NSS
field, and *"Copy it, never compute it."* on totals. Vague descriptions
measurably underperformed enumerated, concrete ones.

### Out of scope: CURP and INFONAVIT credits

Neither is needed for the compliance checks this project performs. Every field
requested from the model is additional surface for hallucination, so a smaller
schema is a reliability decision as much as a scope decision.

---

## Evaluation methodology

Ground truth for the current fixtures was transcribed by hand from the source
documents. It is intentionally **not generated by the model being evaluated** —
using the same model to produce both an extraction and its expected answer
would make the evaluation circular and always score 100%.

```text
Source Document
      │
      ▼
Human Ground Truth ───────────────┐
                                  │
Source Document                   │
      │                           │
      ▼                           ▼
LLM Extraction ───────────► Field Comparison
                                  │
                                  ▼
                         Accuracy + Mismatches
```

Documents are flattened into path-addressable leaf fields:

```text
locations[0].employees[1].socialSecurityNumber
locations[0].employees[1].dailyBaseSalary
locations[0].statedSubtotal
statedTotal
```

Comparison rules, stated explicitly because they affect the reported number:

- Text is trimmed and internal whitespace collapsed before comparison.
- Numeric values use an explicit `0.01` tolerance.
- Fields present in ground truth but absent from the extraction count as
  failures; fields extracted but absent from ground truth are reported as
  invented rows.
- Model-generated `anomalies` prose is excluded from field accuracy, since it
  is model judgment rather than an objective property of the source.

---

## Results

Across the current three controlled fixtures, text-route extraction:

| Metric | Result |
|---|---:|
| Documents | 3 |
| Evaluated fields | 84 |
| Correct fields | 84 |
| Field-level accuracy | **100%** |

These results apply only to this controlled fixture set and should not be read
as production accuracy across arbitrary IMSS/SUA documents.

| Fixture | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Clean document | Baseline structured extraction |
| Inconsistent totals | Verify deterministic reconciliation |
| Malformed layout | Test reconstruction, anomalies, structural edge cases |

---

## Text vs. vision experiment

### Route A — PDF text layer

The text layer is extracted with `pdfjs-dist` and sent to the model as plain
text. On the current fixtures: 100% field accuracy, repeatable output,
significantly fewer input tokens, no image rendering, and no dependency on the
model's vision capability.

The trade-off is that text extraction destroys spatial layout. Column positions
and row alignment do not survive the conversion.

### Route B — PDF pages as images

Each page is rendered to PNG and sent to a vision-capable model, preserving
visual structure. Testing showed this route is **highly dependent on model
tier**.

**Haiku 4.5** on the malformed fixture produced serious failures: character
confusion, transposed contribution columns, a hallucinated employee
reconstructed from a split row's amounts, schema-invalid output, and different
results across repeated runs.

Most importantly, printed subtotal and grand-total values were still extracted
correctly while employee-level data was wrong. That demonstrates a limitation
worth stating plainly:

> Internal reconciliation can pass even when the underlying extraction contains
> fabricated data. Deterministic validation is necessary, but it cannot prove
> extraction fidelity by itself.

**Sonnet 5** on the same document reached 100% field accuracy. Across three
identical runs all 25 structured fields were byte-identical, while the
free-text anomaly descriptions differed each time.

This changed the original conclusion. The experiment does not show that vision
extraction is inherently unreliable — it shows that vision extraction must be
evaluated for the specific model tier in use.

### Resolution is not the lever

Rendering the same page at scale 2 and scale 4 produced different local PNG
sizes (52 KB vs 120 KB) but **identical input token counts** (3,044 both
times). The additional resolution is normalized downstream, so raising render
scale bought nothing measurable in this experiment.

---

## What the experiments changed

Several design decisions came from disproved assumptions rather than being
chosen in advance.

**Deterministic validation is not enough.** A document can be internally
consistent and still be extracted incorrectly. Correct totals do not prove
individual rows are correct.

**Ground truth is required.** Validation checks business consistency; ground
truth checks extraction fidelity. Those are separate responsibilities.

**Model explanations are not verified reasoning.** During testing the model
produced an anomaly explanation that confidently asserted a numeric mismatch
while showing numbers that disproved its own claim in the same sentence. The
deterministic validator returned the correct arithmetic in two lines. Model
prose is treated as informative, never authoritative.

**Do not compare model-generated prose.** Three identical Sonnet vision runs
produced identical structured fields with different `anomalies` wording.
Evaluation therefore operates on schema fields only.

**Treat model changes as system changes.** The same route produced dramatically
different results across tiers. Swapping a model does not preserve extraction
behavior.

---

## Reliability strategy

```text
                ┌─────────────────────┐
                │   Source Document   │
                └──────────┬──────────┘
                           ▼
                ┌─────────────────────┐
                │   LLM Extraction    │
                └──────────┬──────────┘
                           ▼
                ┌─────────────────────┐
                │  Structured Schema  │
                └──────────┬──────────┘
              ┌────────────┴────────────┐
              ▼                         ▼
    Deterministic Validation      Ground-Truth Evaluation
              │                         │
              ▼                         ▼
       Business consistency       Extraction fidelity
              └────────────┬────────────┘
                           ▼
                    Human Review
```

No individual layer is sufficient on its own.

---

## Capabilities

**Structured extraction** — tool-use based schema-forced output, exact
transcription instructions, explicit `missingFields`, explicit anomaly
reporting with confidence levels, and independently testable text and vision
paths.

**Deterministic validation** — two-level reconciliation (employees → location
subtotal → grand total), RFC / NSS / period format checks, duplicate NSS
detection, range and sanity checks, low-confidence inference escalation, and
malformed-extraction detection.

**Evaluation** — human-written ground truth, field-level accuracy,
path-addressed mismatch reporting, detection of missing or invented rows,
tolerance-based numeric comparison, documented text normalization, and disk
caching so iterating on comparison logic does not re-spend API calls.

---

