## Getting started

Requires Node.js, npm and an Anthropic API key.

```bash
git clone https://github.com/NeyhdaNavarro/docucomply-ai.git
cd docucomply-ai
npm install
cp .env.example .env      # add your ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
```

```bash
npm run extract -- fixtures/sample-01.txt        # extract and validate
npm run eval                                     # field-level evaluation
npm run make-pdfs                                # generate PDF fixtures
npm run show-text -- fixtures/pdf/sample-03.pdf  # inspect a PDF text layer
npm run vision -- fixtures/pdf/sample-03.pdf 2   # vision route, optional scale
```

---

## Known limitations

**Validation cannot detect a faithful-looking wrong extraction.** The validator
only catches internal inconsistency. If the model misreads a figure and the
arithmetic still reconciles, validation passes silently. The source document is
never part of the comparison, so this blind spot is structural rather than a
bug — and it is the reason ground-truth measurement exists in this project.

**Monetary values use floating point with tolerance.** TypeScript handles
decimal numbers, but its `number` type is IEEE 754 double-precision. Unlike C#,
it has no native `decimal` type for exact-precision amounts, so representation
artifacts appear — `0.1 + 0.2` evaluates to `0.30000000000000004`. Comparisons
use a `0.01` tolerance so that a difference caused purely by floating point
does not surface as a validation error. A production financial system should
avoid floating point for monetary arithmetic entirely, either representing
amounts as integer cents or using an exact decimal arithmetic library.

**Anomaly reporting reflects the model's threshold, not mine.** On the
malformed fixture the model reported a split row and a stray identifier in the
subtotal line, but did not flag two run-together amounts it also had to
separate.

This is an experimental portfolio project. It is **not intended for production
payroll, tax, legal or regulatory decisions.** Results come from a small
controlled fixture set and are meant to evaluate architecture and failure modes
rather than establish production accuracy.

